Standard Treatment Guidelines in Ayurveda on Dermatological Disorders DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS5,6 (Table 2) Condition Differential Features Investigations Psoriasis • Chronic plaques with sharply demarcated edge,

• Thick silvery scales typically on extensor elbows, knees, scalp;

• Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding when scale removed) positive, nail pitting, possible arthritis;

• Skin biopsy showing acanthosis, parakeratosis,

• Munro micro abscesses. Scabies • Intense nocturnal pruritus with small erythematous papules, vesicles, burrows (thin wavy lines), often on finger webs, wrists, waist, genitalia;

• Excoriations, secondary impetiginization;

• Family members frequently affected.

• Skin scraping with KOH or mineral oil showing mites, eggs, or feces. Tinea corporis • Annular erythematous plaques with raised scaly advancing margins and central clearing, possible vesicles at periphery;

• Worse in humid weather

• Usually asymmetrical;

• History of pets or communal sports.

• KOH mount positive for septate branching hyphae. Drug eruptions • Rapid onset of widespread symmetric erythematous macules, papules,, commonly starting on trunk, with recent drug exposure;

• May involve mucosa if severe.

• Clinical history;

• skin biopsy shows superficial perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate. Lichen planus • Pruritic, purple, polygonal, planar papules with Wickham striae (white lacy lines),

• Commonly on flexor wrists, lumbar area, ankles;

• Oral lesions appear as reticulate white patches;

• May have nail thinning or longitudinal ridging.

