Standard Treatment Guidelines in Ayurveda on Dermatological Disorders Type of Dermatitis Diagnostic Criteria Personal history of other atopic disease (or history of any atopic disease in a first-degree relative of children under 4 years) Visible flexural dermatitis (or dermatitis of cheeks, forehead, or outer limbs in children below 4 years) Irritant Contact Dermatitis • Diagnosis by exclusion: history of repeated irritant exposure, lesions confined to contact area, immediate burning more than itching, improves on avoidance;

• No positive patch test. Allergic Contact Dermatitis • Requires positive patch test (type IV hypersensitivity);

• Pruritic eczematous lesions often extending beyond contact site, delayed onset (24–72h after exposure). Seborrheic Dermatitis • Clinical diagnosis: chronic erythematous patches with greasy yellow scales on seborrheic areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, chest);

• Recurs with stress or winter. Nummular Dermatitis • Circular or oval pruritic eczematous plaques on extremities, frequently oozing;

• Diagnosis supported by negative KOH to rule out tinea. Dyshidrotic Dermatitis • Characteristic presentation of recurrent deep-seated vesicles on sides of fingers, palms, soles, intensely pruritic, often triggered by stress or seasonal factors. An accurate diagnosis is crucial in a disease condition to provide the optimum treatment. In each case of Vicharchika, the Dosha condition has to be analysed along with identification of the exact cause. Appropriate medicines are to be selected based on the cause and the involvement of doshas. External applications are generally indicated if the Dosha is situated in the Twak.

The acute stage is generally considered as a ‘Vata Paittika’ predominant condition, while the sub-acute stage is ‘Pitta Kaphaja’, and the chronic stage is ‘Vata Kaphaja’. The chronic stage may be considered as Kitibha rather than Vicharchika. Evaluation and Assessment21,22,23 The most used tools for assessing dermatitis severity in adult patients are presented and compared.

